WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.147
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.147 --> 00:00:05.633
<v Examiner>Identify the three articles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi,

3
00:00:05.633 --> 00:00:11.531
<v Examiner>and explain how one of the five principles guides a nurse's practice with Māori patients

4
00:00:11.531 --> 00:00:12.269
<v Examiner>and whānau.

5
00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:14.978
The articles, from Lisa's slide.

6
00:00:14.978 --> 00:00:17.591
Article one, kāwanatanga: governance.

7
00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:23.311
Māori are involved in decision-making, at a personal level and at a policy level.

8
00:00:23.311 --> 00:00:28.184
Article two, tino rangatiratanga: self-determination, Māori autonomy.

9
00:00:28.184 --> 00:00:33.762
Māori control over their knowledge, language and customs, recognised as taonga.

10
00:00:33.762 --> 00:00:40.118
Article three, ōritetanga: equity for Māori, the same access and opportunity as non-Māori,

11
00:00:40.118 --> 00:00:41.954
and equal health outcomes.

12
00:00:42.404 --> 00:00:43.551
The principles.

13
00:00:43.551 --> 00:00:47.835
The three Ps, partnership, participation and protection,

14
00:00:47.835 --> 00:00:51.430
came from 1986 and are now considered outdated.

15
00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:56.479
In 2019 the Waitangi Tribunal said they oversimplified the treaty.

16
00:00:56.479 --> 00:00:58.927
Use the five principles instead.

17
00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:01.986
Tino rangatiratanga, self-determination.

18
00:01:01.986 --> 00:01:03.593
Mana taurite, equity.

19
00:01:03.593 --> 00:01:06.346
Whakamarumarutia, active protection.

20
00:01:06.346 --> 00:01:07.876
Kōwhiringa, options.

21
00:01:07.876 --> 00:01:10.018
And pātuitanga, partnership.

22
00:01:10.468 --> 00:01:11.496
Now explain one.

23
00:01:11.496 --> 00:01:13.101
Take kōwhiringa, options.

24
00:01:13.101 --> 00:01:15.221
It means Māori have real choices,

25
00:01:15.221 --> 00:01:19.395
including kaupapa Māori services and culturally appropriate care.

26
00:01:19.395 --> 00:01:23.762
In practice the nurse tells the patient and whānau what's available,

27
00:01:23.762 --> 00:01:27.487
including a kaupapa Māori provider or a Māori health team,

28
00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:31.212
supports them to involve whānau and tikanga in their care,

29
00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:33.396
and respects the choice they make.

30
00:01:33.396 --> 00:01:38.598
Or take mana taurite, equity: the nurse notices who gets poorer outcomes and why,

31
00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:43.543
then removes the barrier, like arranging transport or an earlier appointment.

32
00:01:43.993 --> 00:01:46.448
And cultural safety, kawa whakaruruhau.

33
00:01:46.448 --> 00:01:49.469
Cultural awareness is knowing differences exist.

34
00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:51.420
Sensitivity is respecting them.

35
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.895
Safety goes further: it starts with the nurse reflecting on their own culture and bias,

36
00:01:56.895 --> 00:02:01.679
and it's the patient, not the nurse, who decides whether the care felt safe.

37
00:02:02.129 --> 00:02:07.437
Te Tiriti articles: one, kāwanatanga, governance; two, tino rangatiratanga,

38
00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:10.763
autonomy and taonga; three, ōritetanga, equity.

39
00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:16.495
Five principles: tino rangatiratanga, mana taurite, whakamarumarutia, kōwhiringa,

40
00:02:16.495 --> 00:02:17.274
pātuitanga.

41
00:02:17.274 --> 00:02:19.114
The three Ps are outdated.

42
00:02:19.114 --> 00:02:22.865
Cultural safety: the patient decides if care is safe.
